WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.247
Narrator: There's a cool thing happening

00:00:04.247 --> 00:00:07.020
in this scene in Jordan Peele's "Nope."

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.796
You may notice it starts out pretty dark,

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.050
then slowly gets brighter.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.140
Here, you can't see the logo on OJ's hat,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.880
but two minutes later in a
nearly identical shot, you can.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.770
It's an incredibly subtle trick

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.070
that cinematographer Hoyte
Van Hoytema used to emulate

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.100
how our eyes naturally
respond to darkness.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.000
We build up sort of
fades-in that are very slow

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.240
but that simulate very
much your pupil dilations.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.280
Narrator: But what makes
this subtle trick possible

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.000
is that this nighttime scene
was shot in the daytime.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.030
Day for night is a technique
Hollywood has used for years,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.250
but it hasn't always
produced convincing results

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.950
or clearly visible ones, as seen in shows

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.300
like "House of the Dragon."

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.603
So, how did Hollywood get to this point?

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.950
Here you might be wondering:
Why wouldn't filmmakers

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.347
just film their nighttime scenes at night?

00:01:02.347 --> 00:01:03.563
[chuckles]

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.510
And in reality, many do,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.520
taking advantage of artificial lighting

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.520
and today's more sensitive
digital camera sensors.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.740
But neither modern digital camera sensors

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.320
nor traditional film have the sensitivity

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.050
of the human eye in low-light scenarios.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.850
That means that often, when night scenes

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.770
are filmed in natural lighting conditions,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.140
very little of the shot is visible.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.880
This is why Hollywood often films

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.530
what's called day for night,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.750
shooting nighttime scenes in daylight

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.250
and using cinematic techniques

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.590
to create the illusion of darkness.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.530
François Truffaut even paid
homage to the technique

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.657
in a movie called "Day for Night."

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.750
One standard way to shoot day for night

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.550
is by filming against a blue screen

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.103
and creating a totally CG environment.

00:01:47.103 --> 00:01:49.410
But many filmmakers, like
Jordan Peele on "Nope,"

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.180
want the grounded reality of shooting

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.310
as much as possible out in the field.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.480
When shooting on location,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.100
traditional day-for-night techniques

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.560
typically involve manipulating
the color grading,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.560
adding a bluish tint, and
upping the contrast on the image

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.418
until it resembles night,

00:02:03.418 --> 00:02:05.851
or using smoke, dust, or sand

00:02:05.851 --> 00:02:07.980
and creating fake silhouettes.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.020
The most famous examples
of these techniques

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.600
all shared a similar challenge as "Nope":

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.150
filming on location in
vast desert landscapes,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.730
capturing expanses of
land and sky that are just

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.070
too big to light when filming at night.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.720
But the combination of these techniques

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.570
can create a slightly artificial look,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:28.680
one that Jordan and Hoyte
referred to as "movie nights."

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.600
They noticed the movie-nights aesthetic

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.520
in their visual references for "Nope,"

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.200
including older Westerns

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.960
and the 1962 film "Lawrence of Arabia."

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.550
There's big day-for-night sequences

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.223
in "Lawrence of Arabia,"
and as great and as grand --

00:02:41.223 --> 00:02:44.400
it's people on camels and
wide, vast landscapes --

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.460
and we realized very quickly
that we wanted that scope,

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.170
but we didn't want that look.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.090
Narrator: But to go beyond
the limited aesthetic

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.890
of traditional day-for-night filming,

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.260
they'd have to get much
more technical and creative.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.070
Their goal was to capture the way

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:02.850
the human eye sees at night,
not the way a camera sees,

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.000
like the way our eyes
gradually adjust to darkness.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.988
Stare at this dark shape --

00:03:07.988 --> 00:03:10.350
or, for that matter, just imagine it.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.780
Turns out, just thinking about dark spaces

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.940
can start to dilate our pupils

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.030
so that we can take in
more visual information.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.210
Like "Nope," "Mad Max: Fury
Road" emulated this transition

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:24.120
subtly by starting its
night scene out pretty dark

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.210
to sell the night, then
slowly making it brighter.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.920
Once our eyes are adjusted,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.840
there's the question of how to replicate

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.870
the level of fine detail that
we can perceive at night.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.300
Productions often struggle
to nail this down,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.610
especially because when
filming day for night,

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:42.930
DPs usually underexpose the
scenes by three or four stops,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.360
planning to make them as dark as possible.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.700
However, underexposed day-for-night scenes

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.350
are often rendered so dark

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.180
to compensate for the daytime

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.670
that you can barely make
out anything at all.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.550
Audiences have complained
about this limited visibility

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.190
in modern TV shows like
"House of the Dragon,"

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:00.690
which was shot in full day

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:04.023
and then darkened -- a lot -- in post.

00:04:04.023 --> 00:04:06.450
Selective use of light
can help make scenes

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.400
more legible to viewers.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.860
In "Pan's Labyrinth," DP Guillermo Navarro

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.810
was going for an eerie effect,

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.970
so he drilled tiny holes in the sets

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.160
to allow some light to leak in.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.010
In the giant-frog scene,
he lit Ofelia's face

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.820
with a fiber-optic light
attached directly to the camera.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.317
Other films have gone
the opposite direction.

00:04:26.317 --> 00:04:29.340
"Mad Max" wanted viewers to
be able to follow every shot

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.247
of its big nighttime set piece,

00:04:31.247 --> 00:04:33.960
so the team massively
overexposed the photography

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.290
to ensure they captured all
the detail in the shadows.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.060
The effect worked well on "Mad Max"

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.460
because of the dynamic range

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.568
of the digital cameras used on the film

00:04:42.568 --> 00:04:44.684
and because of the stylized night look

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:45.707
that they were going for,

00:04:45.707 --> 00:04:47.423
which was supposed to look realistic

00:04:47.423 --> 00:04:49.081
but not photorealistic,

00:04:49.081 --> 00:04:53.047
so they could afford to make
it less dark and more blue.

00:04:53.047 --> 00:04:56.160
"Mad Max" also used a
lot of sky replacements.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.520
And skies are in fact
often the biggest giveaways

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.200
when filming day for night.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.060
When a scene's darkened in post,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.130
the skies often still look bright

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:06.690
relative to the rest of the shot,

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.060
so we can feel the day leak through.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.820
To create authentic night skies on "Nope,"

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.700
Hoyte pulled from earlier
work on "Ad Astra,"

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.860
where he had experimented
with infrared cameras

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.051
to create the black skies
of the moon chase sequence.

00:05:20.051 --> 00:05:22.095
Hoyte: There's very little
ambient light on the moon,

00:05:22.095 --> 00:05:24.660
so it's a very weird
hybrid of day and night.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.050
We needed to shoot a rover
chase on the moon's surface,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:31.830
which is a gigantic area,
something we couldn't light up.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.780
So you end up with a black-and-white image

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:36.172
that has very good light levels

00:05:36.172 --> 00:05:39.510
and a light relationship
between lightness and darkness.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.000
But then you have to figure
out a way to get color back,

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.520
and you could think about
coloring it by hand,

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.860
but we were thinking, "Well,
we are shooting this on film.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.350
What if we could combine
those two cameras together

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.960
and then just mix the color back in

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.520
selectively where we want it?"

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.340
You use one camera to
tell you the relationship

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.410
of the light levels between everything,

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.030
and then you use the other camera

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.020
to gather color information
and film grain and such things.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.664
Narrator: By shooting natural
sunlight with infrared cameras

00:06:06.664 --> 00:06:09.105
and then giving it a
slight contrast boost,

00:06:09.105 --> 00:06:11.139
you end up with images
that are brightly lit

00:06:11.139 --> 00:06:12.755
but with dark skies,

00:06:12.755 --> 00:06:14.738
which worked perfectly for "Nope."

00:06:14.738 --> 00:06:15.960
Let's go, boy!

00:06:17.555 --> 00:06:18.731
Yeah!

00:06:18.731 --> 00:06:21.510
Narrator: But this time, Hoyte
had to restructure the rig

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.210
for the super-heavy large-format cameras

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.280
that he and Jordan were working with.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.050
Hoyte: So we ramped it up
because this one we shot

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.990
on 65-millimeter film, so we had to

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.230
rebuild the rig for a
65-millimeter-film camera.

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:36.240
Narrator: While the cameras
were ready to shoot,

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.820
the rig was heavy and pretty unbalanced.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.590
And a lot of the night sequences in "Nope"

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.480
were major action set pieces,

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.450
where the crew had to follow
running horses and actors

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:46.455
and couldn't be limited

00:06:46.455 --> 00:06:48.421
in how they could move the rig around.

00:06:48.421 --> 00:06:49.654
So they stabilized the rig

00:06:49.654 --> 00:06:52.950
by attaching it to a specially
retrofitted camera car.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.350
Hoyte: A lot of the action
that we shot over the field,

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.990
we shot from an instrument
called the Edge.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.280
It's a car with an arm,

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.030
which has a very big
stabilized head on there.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.665
Narrator: The rig also allowed
for a more dynamic shoot

00:07:05.665 --> 00:07:07.302
by addressing one of the limitations

00:07:07.302 --> 00:07:08.940
of traditional day for night:

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.853
needing the sun exactly in
the right place when filming.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:15.090
Typically, depending on the time of day,

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.872
the sun can cast harsh,
overly dramatic shadows

00:07:17.872 --> 00:07:19.560
that filmmakers want to avoid.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.600
If you do traditional day for night,

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.040
the quality of the image

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.530
is so dependent on your light direction.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.320
Day for night works very
nice if you're backlit.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:31.140
It works not very well if you're frontlit.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:32.970
Narrator: Typically,
filmmakers get around this

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.623
by carefully positioning the actors

00:07:34.623 --> 00:07:36.060
with their backs to the sun

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.910
to create a rim-light effect
that resembles moonlight.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.290
Hoyte: With the infrared rig,

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.270
we found that it was much easier.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.820
We didn't have to be as
precious about light direction

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.520
as you traditionally need
to be for day for night.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.522
Narrator: In post, the VFX team merged

00:07:49.522 --> 00:07:52.410
the digital infrared footage
with the color film footage

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.090
in a process that almost resembles

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.280
colorizing an old black-and-white movie,

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.440
except instead of inventing the colors,

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.230
they could extract them from
the film-camera photography,

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:04.702
which captured all the vital
color and texture information.

00:08:04.702 --> 00:08:06.935
Run, OJ! Run!

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.230
Narrator: This allowed
the filmmakers on "Nope"

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:13.320
to capture more detail --
important for how Jordan

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.520
wanted the audience to
experience the movie.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.990
Instead of pushing the
thriller forward by relying

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:22.050
on the blunt force of the
dark night and sound effects,

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:23.670
he wanted to encourage the audience

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.440
to actively look into the darkness

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.450
and start seeing things for themselves.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.400
Hoyte: Traditionally,
cinematographers hide things

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.590
in the shadow, and there's darkness,

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:34.620
but you see just what the
cinematographer needs you to see.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.917
But here, we wanted, at
first, to put you at a place

00:08:37.917 --> 00:08:39.720
[rumbling]

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.540
and give you that scope,
and then within that,

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.167
sort of play with these little elements.

00:08:45.167 --> 00:08:48.250
[rumbling]

00:08:49.373 --> 00:08:51.420
Yeah, nah, nah, nah.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.370
Narrator: Combining the
color with the infrared

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.160
also helped the team nail
the correct relationships

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.470
between light and dark parts of each shot.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.360
We were, of course, being very analytical

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:04.050
about how your eye perceives
different colors at night.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.810
The way that, for
instance, foliage and green

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.680
is much brighter at night
than a lot of other colors.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.620
The fact that the light on ground level

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:17.287
is very often brighter than
what you get from the sky.

00:09:17.287 --> 00:09:19.320
The sky plays a very big role in "Nope,"

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.110
so we also want to be able to feel the sky

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:24.330
and feel the differences in density

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.700
in where there's overcast,
where there's clouds,

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.250
or where there's moonlight,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.060
Narrator: In post, the team
sprinkled in additional details

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:33.776
to emphasize the full richness

00:09:33.776 --> 00:09:35.342
and depth of the night scenes.

00:09:35.342 --> 00:09:37.140
Hoyte: Visual effects went back at night,

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.320
shot very specific plates
of practical lights

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.170
in the distance to then
add it to those pictures.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.510
And for me, these little things are really

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.490
the cherry toppings on the cake.

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.830
It totally completes it
and sort of finishes it.

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:51.210
Looking up at the mountain ridge,

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:52.850
there's these telegraph poles,

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:55.650
and they have these tiny little
red blinking lights on them.

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:58.530
And in your monochromatic view of night,

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.630
you see those little red dots blinking,

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.490
and suddenly, you're looking at a picture

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:06.480
that has full saturation
to it and full depth.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.460
Narrator: But one last piece was missing.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:09.930
Hoyte: This is shot in California,

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.820
and you get very little clouds.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.730
The story of "Nope" is a very
specific cat-and-mouse game

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.130
in which Jean Jacket is hiding in clouds

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.160
and moving from one cloud to the other.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.840
Narrator: So the VFX team
added digital cloudscapes

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.590
created from modular sets of
hundreds of 3D-modeled clouds,

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:27.000
altered to match the light

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:29.220
of the combined infrared-color footage.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.490
The VFX team also added
drifting shadows of the clouds

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.260
to connect the sky with the ground.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:35.817
And in "Nope," this tech

00:10:35.817 --> 00:10:38.490
and partnership between
camera and visual effects

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:40.620
made the movie nights feel every bit

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.140
as real, immersive, and expansive

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.590
as how Jordan and Hoyte
experienced them on location.

